<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richard Francis Burton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1890">1890</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sir Richard Burton</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Richard Francis Burton was a British explorer, soldier, linguist and writer. In 1855 he became the first European known to enter the walled city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a>, and in 1857–1858 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hanning-speke">John Hanning Speke</a> explored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>. He had made a disguised pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 1853, and he later translated the Arabian Nights and the Kama Sutra.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-francis-burton.jpg" alt="Richard Francis Burton" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rischgitz/Stringer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richard_Francis_Burton_by_Rischgitz,_1864.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
